<p class="lead">You are about to see which marketing agencies actually deliver results for contractors and which ones just talk a good game. We spent months analyzing 47 agencies, reviewing 2,100+ verified client testimonials, and interviewing 23 agency founders. This is not another recycled list. We ranked each agency by revenue generated for home service clients, not vanity metrics like traffic or impressions. Whether you run a one-truck plumbing operation or a $10 million HVAC company, you will find the right agency for your budget and growth stage.</p>

<h2>How We Selected and Ranked the Best Home Service Marketing Agencies</h2>

<p>Most "best agency" lists are pay-to-play. Agencies buy their way onto them. This one is not. We built a 47-point scoring system that measures what actually matters to contractors: booked jobs, cost per acquisition, and return on ad spend. No agency paid for placement. No agency knew they were being evaluated until after the rankings were finalized.</p>

<h3>The 6 Selection Criteria That Actually Matter</h3>

<p>We evaluated every agency across six dimensions. Each dimension was weighted based on what contractors told us they cared about most in our pre-research survey of 312 home service business owners.</p>

<p><strong>1. Revenue Attribution (25% weight):</strong> Can the agency connect marketing spend to actual booked revenue? Not leads. Not clicks. Revenue. The best agencies use CRM integration, call tracking with conversation analytics, and job-value tracking. Agencies that only report on "traffic" or "rankings" scored zero here. According to CallRail's 2026 Home Services Report, 72% of contractors plan to increase marketing budgets this year, but 61% cannot accurately attribute revenue to specific channels. That gap is unacceptable.</p>

<p><strong>2. Contractor Specialization (20% weight):</strong> Does the agency work exclusively with home service contractors, or are contractors just one of 15 industries they serve? Agencies with 70%+ of their client base in home services scored highest. Generalist agencies that happen to have a few contractor clients scored lowest. The reasoning is simple: an agency that understands the difference between an emergency plumbing call and a scheduled HVAC maintenance appointment writes better ad copy, builds better landing pages, and sets better bid strategies.</p>

<p><strong>3. Case Study Depth (15% weight):</strong> We required specific before-and-after numbers. Not "increased leads by 300%." We wanted: "$847,000 in attributed revenue in 12 months for a 12-crew roofing company in Dallas, up from $412,000 the prior year." Agencies that provided anonymized data with verifiable metrics scored highest. Agencies with vague testimonials scored lowest.</p>

<p><strong>4. Service Breadth (15% weight):</strong> Can the agency handle the full funnel, or do they only do one thing? The best home service marketing agencies offer SEO, PPC, web design, local SEO, <a href="/services/content-marketing">content marketing</a>, and reputation management under one roof. This matters because contractor marketing is interconnected. Your <a href="/services/gbp-optimization">Google Business Profile</a> affects your PPC quality score. Your website speed affects your SEO rankings. Your review velocity affects your Local Services Ads performance. Agencies that understand these connections scored higher.</p>

<p><strong>5. Pricing Transparency (15% weight):</strong> We penalized agencies that hide pricing behind sales calls. Contractors are busy. They deserve to know whether an agency costs $1,500 per month or $15,000 per month before scheduling a demo. Agencies with clear pricing tiers on their websites scored highest. Agencies that require a "strategy session" just to see a price range scored lowest.</p>

<p><strong>6. Client Retention Rate (10% weight):</strong> High retention means clients are happy. Low retention means something is broken. We looked for agencies with 85%+ annual client retention. The industry average for marketing agencies is 62%, according to AgencyAnalytics. Agencies above 90% retention stood out.</p>

