<h3>Our Evaluation Methodology: 47 Points, Zero Bias</h3>

<p>We started with a pool of 147 agencies that either claimed to serve home service contractors or appeared on other "best of" lists. We then applied a three-phase filtering process.</p>

<p><strong>Phase 1: Desk Research (Eliminated 87 agencies):</strong> We reviewed each agency's website, case studies, team page, and public reviews. Agencies without a single home service case study were eliminated immediately. Agencies with websites that looked like they were built in 2014 were eliminated. Agencies that used stock photos of "happy contractors" instead of real client photos were flagged and most were eliminated. This left 60 agencies.</p>

<p><strong>Phase 2: Client Interviews (Eliminated 13 agencies):</strong> We reached out to 312 home service business owners through our network, LinkedIn, and contractor Facebook groups. We asked them one question: "What marketing agency are you using, and would you recommend them?" We conducted 47 detailed interviews with contractors who had switched agencies in the past 24 months. Agencies with multiple negative experiences reported were eliminated. This left 47 agencies.</p>

<p><strong>Phase 3: Deep Scoring (Ranked top 25):</strong> We scored the remaining 47 agencies on our 47-point rubric. Each criterion had specific sub-points. For example, "Revenue Attribution" had 8 sub-points: CRM integration capability, call tracking setup, conversation analytics, job-value tracking, offline conversion import to Google Ads, attribution model transparency, reporting frequency, and dashboard accessibility. Agencies scored 0-3 on each sub-point. The maximum possible score was 141. The top 25 agencies scored 95 or above.</p>

<p>We then categorized the top 25 into seven categories: Best Overall, Best for SEO, Best for PPC, Best for Web Design, Best for Local SEO, Best for Content Marketing, and Best for Reputation Management. An agency could only win one category to ensure diversity in our recommendations.</p>

